\source{
National Center for Education Statistics. National Education Longitudinal Study of 1988 (NELS:88): Public-use data files, base year (1988) and first follow-up (1990). U.S. Department of Education. \url{https://nces.ed.gov/surveys/nels88/data_products.asp}

Teaching extract of 2,000 students prepared for UC Berkeley GSE courses (EDUC 293A/275B), about 2004. Provenance: verified.
}
\usage{
nels_data
}
\description{
Questionnaire items, composites and test scores for 2,000 students in the
National Education Longitudinal Study of 1988 (NELS:88): 299 variables
from the base year, 1988 (grade 8), and the first follow-up, 1990
(grade 10). One row per student. The NELS variables keep their original
names and codes, for example \code{bys12} (sex) and \code{by2xmstd} (grade-8
mathematics score). The file is a teaching extract of the NCES
public-use data; how its 2,000 students were chosen is not documented.
Because there are 299 variables, use \code{codebook("nels_data")} to search
variables in R, or scroll below the variable list for \strong{Details} and
\strong{Examples}.
}
\details{
\strong{Layout.} The variables are grouped by wave and kind: the base-year
(1988) student questionnaire (148 items, \code{bys2a} to \code{bys83j}), tests,
composites and school characteristics; then the first follow-up (1990)
questionnaire (105 items, \code{f1s8a} to \code{f1s110yr}), one test score, and
composites and status variables such as \code{f1stat}. The only 1990 test score
is reading (\code{f12xrstd}), so change in achievement can be followed for
reading only. \code{id} is the NELS student identifier (\code{STU_ID} in NCES files).
\code{year1} and \code{year2} are not NELS variables and their meaning is not
documented; leave them out of analyses.

\strong{Codes.} Items keep their NELS codes and follow the NCES NELS:88 codebook
wording, so check them before modeling. The 40 agreement items run from
1 = strongly agree to 4 = strongly disagree. Most yes/no items are 1 = yes
and 2 = no, but \code{bys74} (held back a grade) is 1 = no and 2 = yes. \code{bys12}
(sex) is 1 = male and 2 = female, and \code{bys31a} (race/ethnicity) runs from
1 = Asian or Pacific Islander to 5 = American Indian. Nominal items, such
as occupations and school programs, need \code{factor()}. \code{codebook("nels_data")}
returns the full variable table, with codes and units, as a data frame.

\strong{Missing data and version 1.0.0.} In 0.2.0 most nonresponse codes were
stored as numbers, so a 98 or a 99.98 could enter a model as if it were
a real value. Version 1.0.0 recodes them to NA, 41,966 values in all
(0.2.0 had already recoded 82 others, the 998 codes in \code{g8minor} and
\code{g8lunch}):
\itemize{
\item NELS nonresponse codes such as 8 = missing, 9 = legitimate skip,
6 = multiple response and 7 = refusal, or 98, 99, 96 and 97 on items
with more categories (40,145 cells);
\item non-integer codes from the NELS codebook in test scores and
composites, such as 99.98 = missing and 99.99 = test not completed
(1,356 cells);
\item "don't know" answers on the ordered items \code{bypared}, \code{bys34a},
\code{bys34b} and \code{f1s17} (465 cells).
}

On nominal items, such as the occupation and school-program items,
"don't know" is kept as its own category. The \code{recoded_to_na} column of
\code{codebook("nels_data")} shows what was recoded in each variable.

Legitimate skips (questions that did not apply to a student) are NA as
well, so NA does not always mean a missing answer: \code{bys31c} and
\code{bys31d}, which ask Hispanic students about their origin and race, hold
1,717 legitimate skips each. The 109 students who did not take part in
the 1990 follow-up (\code{f1stat} other than 0) have no 1990 questionnaire
data. No student is complete on all 299 variables, so
\code{na.omit(nels_data)} returns no rows; choose the variables first.

\strong{Sample.} How the 2,000 students were drawn is not documented, and the
file has no sampling weights, so results describe these students rather
than the national population of eighth graders that NELS:88 sampled.
All 296 named NELS variables (plus \code{id}, the NCES student ID \code{STU_ID}) are
in the NCES public-use data; the remaining two columns (\code{year1}, \code{year2})
are undocumented.

\strong{Suggested analyses.}
\itemize{
\item model building and variable selection, for example for the grade-8
mathematics score (\code{by2xmstd}) with SES (\code{byses}), bracketed homework
time (\code{byhomewk}, 1 = none to 8 = 21+ hours/week), attitudes and school
characteristics as candidate predictors;
\item regression diagnostics (residuals, leverage and influence) for such
models;
\item change in reading between grade 8 and grade 10: \code{f12xrstd} on
\code{by2xrstd} and other predictors;
\item logistic regression of a binary item, such as being held back a grade
(\code{bys74}, recoded to 0/1, e.g. \code{I(bys74 == 2)}).
}
}
\examples{
head(nels_data)

# Find variables by their labels
cb <- codebook("nels_data")
cb[grepl("socioeconomic", cb$label, ignore.case = TRUE),
   c("variable", "label", "group")]

# Grade-8 mathematics score on SES (nonresponse codes are NA since 1.0.0)
fit <- lm(by2xmstd ~ byses, data = nels_data)
summary(fit)

# Reading in 1990 (grade 10) given reading in 1988 (grade 8)
lm(f12xrstd ~ by2xrstd + byses, data = nels_data)
}
